# Level 4 → Level 5

## Concept
This level drops the password into a directory full of decoy files 
(`-file00` through `-file09` ), where only one is actually 
the right text — the rest are junk designed to waste your time. 
The filenames also start with a dash, which is the same shell-parsing 
trap from level 1: a leading `-` can get mistaken for a command flag.

## Commands used
cat ./-file07

## Why it works
The `./` in front of the filename tells the shell explicitly "look in 
the current directory for this," which prevents `-file07` from being 
misread as an option flag on the `cat` command. Once that ambiguity is 
removed, `cat` just prints the file's contents like normal — and that 
one happened to be the readable file holding the password.
